p similar tasks together as much  as possible i'll show you how these  ideas kind of actually manifest in my  schedule a little bit later on so like i  said my deep work sessions are just  built into my routine and these are  things that i don't even really have to  think about doing they're just a fact of  life for me  so you can see this in my calendar um  everything that's routine is in yellow  and yeah that's things like my deep work  sessions my daily review or gym  and my calendar sinks directly into  sensama  which is kind of my home for everything  another side to setting up your routines  is kind of understanding your peak times  we only have five hours in a day when we  can concentrate really hard so we really  need to use this time wisely so for me  that's right in the mornings it's also  when i have the least amount of  distractions but yours might be  different if you don't know when your  peak times are i would just start off by  tracking your days over a period of like  two or three weeks just try to get a  sense of when you're the most productive  so these routines kind of set the  foundation for my weekly schedule  and  it also helps me  not have to spend so much time  actually like planning these things  when it comes to actually scheduling my  weeks i dedicate about an hour every  sunday just to  roughly planning out my week and you can  see that that's also scheduled in my  calendar and so  first of all i try to set like three  goals for the week in the objectives tab  if i finish these three things  i can go out for a beer on friday and  just feel accomplished i also have it in  my calendar to check in every day to see  if i'm on track these little planning  sessions on sunday and throughout the  week is also when i do what i call a  brain dump  our brains are great at  many things  but  it's not so great at remembering things  or keeping track of things  or at least mine isn't and so if i don't  put it down somewhere i'm gonna be  constantly worried that i'm forgetting  something it's really taking up the  limited brain power that i have so every  sunday i try to dump everything that's  on my mind that alone just gives me a  peace of mind then throughout the week  like whenever something pops into my  head i put it down in the same place i  use a combination of sensama and click  up but it can be anything it can be a  notebook or an app but just make sure  that it's something that you actually  like using  because you will have to refer back to  it regularly if you want it to work  after i do my brain dump on my sunday  planning sessions i move into the  processing stage  sometimes when you're just dumping ideas  from your brain and you're just like  trying to capture your thoughts they're  not totally fleshed out into actionable  tasks so i dedicate time during my  sunday planning sessions or at the end  of each day  to just like quickly process them into  concrete actionable tasks  and then actually schedule them into my  calendar if there's anything that's  urgent and you know if there's anything  that can be done in less than two  minutes i just do it without actually  scheduling it into my calendar that way  you can just kind of move on and knock  these down pretty quickly you can  actually put in an estimated time for  each task as well so you can pretty  quickly see if your workload is  realistic or not it integrates with your  calendar so for example my morning deep  work sessions you can see that it's  blocked out you can also link your gmail  to this and turn them into tasks  sometimes you have an email where you  need to respond with like a little bit  more information for example if you're  putting together like a fee proposal  it's gonna take you a little bit more  time to work through that yeah just like  schedule that email as a task here and  drag and drop that into your day i try  to dedicate a chunk of time to just  doing emails you know this is what i  mean by limiting context switching